  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure conditions have begun to slowly erode away some\ncloud cover, which has blossomed fog in areas where skies have\ncleared. However, clouds will likely increase again today, and \nlasting through the day. Overall high pressure conditions will \nkeep conditions dry, with very low to negligible chances for \nprecipitation expected. Warm air advection from southwesterly \nflow will push temperatures into the 70s with upper 60s in the \nCatskills.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA weak warm front will cross through region late overnight\ntonight through tomorrow morning, which will bring a few light \nshowers across the area, but nothing substantial. The next \nchance for more widespread rainfall will come on Friday, when \nthe cold front crosses the region. Scattered showers are \nexpected across the area on Friday, with rainfall amounts around\na quarter to a half inch expected. The front exits the region \nFriday night with Canadian high pressure pushing in behind it. \nTemperatures will be 10 to 15 degrees cooler going into the\nweekend than tomorrow's and Friday's highs, which are \nforecasted to top out in the mid to upper 70s. Overnight lows \ncould get into the low to mid 30s Saturday night across northern\nOneida County, but there remains some uncertainty with cloud \ncover, as another system approaches by early Sunday morning. \n\nThere still remains some timing differences among model \nguidance runs on an upper trough/cold front dropping out of \nCanada Sunday into Monday, and low pressure tracking off the \nMid Atlantic Coast to our south. With uncertainty remaining, we\ncontinued to utilize the NBM's solution for late weekend into\nearly next week's forecast.",
